#: ../../../users/security.rst:2
msgid "Security Principles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:4
msgid ""
"Security is one of the primary project goals. This means that it should not "
"be possible for an attacker to join a cluster uninvited, and it should not "
"be possible to extract private information from intercepted traffic. "
"Currently this is implemented as follows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:9
msgid ""
"All device to device traffic is protected by TLS. To prevent uninvited "
"devices from joining a cluster, the certificate fingerprint of each device "
"is compared to a preset list of acceptable devices at connection "
"establishment. The fingerprint is computed as the SHA-256 hash of the "
"certificate and displayed in a human-friendly encoding, called Device ID."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:15
msgid ""
"Incoming requests for file data are verified to the extent that the "
"requested file name must exist in the local index and the global model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:18
msgid ""
"For information about ensuring you are running the code you think you are "
"and for reporting security vulnerabilities, please see the official "
"`security page <https://syncthing.net/security>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:23
msgid "Information Leakage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:26
msgid "Global Discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:28
msgid ""
"When global discovery is enabled, Syncthing sends an announcement every 30 "
"minutes to the global discovery servers so that they can keep a mapping "
"between your device ID and external IP. The announcement contain the device "
"ID and listening port(s). Also, when connecting to other devices that have "
"not been seen on the local network, a query is sent to the global discovery "
"servers containing the device ID of the requested device. The connection to "
"the discovery server is encrypted using TLS and the discovery server "
"certificate is verified, so the contents of the query should be considered "
"private between the device and the discovery server. The discovery servers "
"are currently hosted by :user:`calmh`. Global discovery defaults to **on**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:39
msgid ""
"When turned off, devices with dynamic addresses not on the local network "
"cannot be found and connected to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:42
msgid ""
"An eavesdropper on the Internet can deduce which machines are running "
"Syncthing with global discovery enabled, and what their device IDs are."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:45
msgid ""
"The operator of the discovery server can map arbitrary device addresses to "
"IP addresses, and deduce which devices are connected to each other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:48
msgid ""
"If a different global discovery server is configured, no data is sent to the "
"default global discovery servers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:52
msgid "Local Discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:54
msgid ""
"When local discovery is enabled, Syncthing sends broadcast (IPv4) and "
"multicast (IPv6) packets to the local network every 30 seconds. The packets "
"contain the device ID and listening port. Local discovery defaults to **on**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:58
msgid ""
"An eavesdropper on the local network can deduce which machines are running "
"Syncthing with local discovery enabled, and what their device IDs are."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:61
msgid ""
"When turned off, devices with dynamic addresses on the local network cannot "
"be found and connected to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:65
msgid "Upgrade Checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:67
msgid ""
"When automatic upgrades are enabled, Syncthing checks for a new version at "
"startup and then once every twelve hours. This is by an HTTPS request to the "
"download site for releases, currently hosted by :user:`calmh`. Automatic "
"upgrades default to **on** (unless Syncthing was compiled with upgrades "
"disabled)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:73
msgid ""
"Even when automatic upgrades are disabled in the configuration, an upgrade "
"check as above is done when the GUI is loaded, in order to show the "
"\"Upgrade to ...\" button when necessary. This can be disabled only by "
"compiling Syncthing with upgrades disabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:78
msgid ""
"The actual download, should an upgrade be available, is done from "
"**GitHub**, thus exposing the user to them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:81
msgid ""
"The upgrade check (or download) requests *do not* contain any identifiable "
"information about the user or device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:85
msgid "Usage Reporting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:87
msgid ""
"When usage reporting is enabled, Syncthing reports usage data at startup and "
"then every 24 hours. The report is sent as an HTTPS POST to the usage "
"reporting server, currently hosted by :user:`calmh`. The contents of the "
"usage report can be seen behind the \"Preview\" link in settings. Usage "
"reporting defaults to **off** but the GUI will ask once about enabling it, "
"shortly after the first install."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:94
msgid ""
"The reported data is protected from eavesdroppers, but the connection to the "
"usage reporting server itself may expose the client as running Syncthing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:98
msgid "Sync Connections (BEP)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:100
msgid ""
"Sync connections are attempted to all configured devices, when the address "
"is possible to resolve. The sync connection is based on TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3. "
"The TLS certificates can be obtained by an eavesdropper, although it is more "
"difficult to do so in TLS 1.3. This means that the contents of the "
"certificate are visible, which includes certificate Common Name (by default "
"``syncthing``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:104
msgid ""
"An eavesdropper can deduce that this is a Syncthing connection and under "
"certain circumstances calculate the device IDs involved based on the hashes "
"of the sent certificates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:107
msgid ""
"Likewise, if the sync port (default 22000) is accessible from the internet, "
"a port scanner may discover it, attempt a TLS negotiation and thus obtain "
"the device certificate. This provides the same information as in the "
"eavesdropper case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:113
msgid "Relay Connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:115
msgid ""
"When relaying is enabled, Syncthing will look up the pool of public relays "
"and establish a connection to one of them (the best, based on an internal "
"heuristic). The selected relay server will learn the connecting device's "
"device ID. Relay servers can be run by **anyone in the general public**. "
"Relaying defaults to **on**. Syncthing can be configured to disable "
"relaying, or only use specific relays."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:122
msgid ""
"If a relay connections is required between two devices, the relay will learn "
"the other device's device ID as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:125
msgid ""
"Any data exchanged between the two devices is encrypted as usual and not "
"subject to inspection by the relay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:129
msgid "Web GUI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:131
msgid ""
"If the web GUI is accessible, it exposes the device as running Syncthing. "
"The web GUI defaults to being reachable from the **local host only**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:135
msgid "In Short"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:137
msgid ""
"Parties doing surveillance on your network (whether that be corporate IT, "
"the NSA or someone else) will be able to see that you use Syncthing, and "
"your device IDs `are OK to share anyway <https://docs.syncthing.net/users/"
"faq.html#should-i-keep-my-device-ids-secret>`__, but the actual transmitted "
"data is protected as well as we can. Knowing your device ID can expose your "
"IP address, using global discovery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:145
msgid "Protecting your Syncthing keys and identity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:147
msgid ""
"Anyone who can access the Syncthing TLS keys and config file on your device "
"can impersonate your device, connect to your peers, and then have access to "
"your synced files. Here are some general principles to protect your files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:151
msgid ""
"If a device of yours is lost, make sure to revoke its access from your other "
"devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/security.rst:153
msgid ""
"If you're syncing confidential data on an encrypted disk to guard against "
"device theft, put the Syncthing config folder on the same encrypted disk to "
"avoid leaking keys and metadata. Or, use whole disk encryption."
msgstr ""
